{
  "id": "binary-tree-preorder-traversal",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-preorder-traversal",
  "slug": "binary-tree-preorder-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Preorder Traversal",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion",
    "traversal"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "preorder means: me first, then everything on the left, then everything on the right. what recursive call order does that suggest?",
    "write a helper dfs(i, result) that appends to a shared result list, given the current node index",
    "the base case is when the index is out of bounds or holds -101 - just return without appending anything",
    "the recursive case is: append tree[i] first, then dfs(2*i+1), then dfs(2*i+2)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "appending the node's value after recursing into children instead of before, which produces postorder instead of preorder",
    "recursing into the right subtree before the left subtree, which produces the wrong left-right order",
    "not checking the -101 sentinel in the base case, only the array bounds, causing sentinel values to leak into the output"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node there\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing and its right child is 4.\n\n**Return the preorder traversal of the tree, as a plain list: visit the current node first, then the entire left subtree, then the entire right subtree, applied recursively.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 1000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree, which returns an empty array",
      "single node tree, which returns a single-element array",
      "a tree that is entirely a left chain or entirely a right chain"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, 2, -101, 1]",
        "output": "[5, 4, 11, 2, 8, 1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[5, 4, 11, 2, 8, 1]",
        "explanation": "root 5 has left child 4 (with children 11, 2) and right child 8 (with only a right child 1). preorder visits 5, then 4's subtree (4, 11, 2), then 8's subtree (8, 1)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, -101, 2, -101, -101, 3, -101]",
        "output": "[1, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "root 1 has no left child, only right child 2; 2's left child is 3. preorder visits 1, then 2's subtree (2, then its left child 3)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-101]",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "an empty tree produces an empty traversal"
      }
    ],
